# Point-in-time correctness

Look-ahead bias is the most common cause of "great backtest, terrible live".
We assume the worst: any code that touches past data is suspect until proven
otherwise.

## Rules

1. The only sanctioned way to attach lagged data to a bar is `asof_join` or
   `asof_lookup` from `app/core/ai/features/asof.py`. They use backward-only
   joins.
2. Every feature has an explicit `version: int`. If you change the body of a
   feature, bump the version. The `FeatureRegistry` enforces this via
   `body_hash`.
3. Pipelines are idempotent: running `pipelines/<x>.py:run(asof=D)` twice for
   the same `D` must yield the same result. Use `UPSERT` on `(symbol, ts)` or
   the table's natural key.
4. Universe membership is point-in-time. `BarReplayer` MUST inner-join against
   `universe_membership` so a delisted symbol disappears from the replay on
   its delist date. No survivorship-skewed lists.
5. Triple-barrier labels carry `t1` (label realisation time). Splitters MUST
   purge any training row with `t1` inside the test window AND embargo a
   margin around it.
6. News + earnings join with a "knowable lag" — assume a 5-minute lag from
   wire timestamp before any model can see a headline.
7. Macro series (FRED) have a publication lag — never join on the data's
   "as_of" date; join on the publication date.

## Smells to reject

- `df[df.ts < ts]` in a feature body — not an asof join
- A model's training set including its own test fold's labels
- A backtest using today's S&P 500 constituents to backtest 2015
- A "simple shift" used as the only protection against look-ahead — also use
  the asof helper
